<div class="docs-prose text-gray-700">
  <!-- Getting Started -->
  <section id="getting-started">
    <h2>Getting Started</h2>
    <p>Welcome to HomeChat! This guide will help you get up and running with your self-hosted chat server.</p>

    <h3>What is HomeChat?</h3>
    <p>HomeChat is a lightweight, self-hosted chat application designed for home networks. It provides real-time messaging, bot integrations, and seamless Home Assistant connectivity - all running on your own hardware.</p>

    <h3>Creating an Account</h3>
    <p>To create an account:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Navigate to the HomeChat homepage</li>
      <li>Click <strong>Sign up</strong></li>
      <li>Enter your desired username and password</li>
      <li>Click <strong>Sign Up</strong></li>
    </ol>
    <p>If your server requires admin approval, you'll see a message indicating your account is pending. An administrator will need to approve your account before you can access the chat.</p>

    <h3>Logging In</h3>
    <p>To sign in to your account:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Go to the sign-in page</li>
      <li>Enter your username and password</li>
      <li>If you have two-factor authentication enabled, you'll be prompted to enter your verification code</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <!-- User Guide: Channels -->
  <section id="channels">
    <h2>Channels</h2>
    <p>Channels are shared spaces where you can communicate with other users. HomeChat supports different types of channels for various use cases.</p>

    <h3>Channel Types</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Public Channels</strong> - Visible to all users. Anyone can join and participate in the conversation.</li>
      <li><strong>Private Channels</strong> - Only visible to members. You must be invited to join a private channel.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Creating a Channel</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Click the <strong>+</strong> button next to "Channels" in the sidebar</li>
      <li>Enter a channel name (2-50 characters)</li>
      <li>Optionally add a description</li>
      <li>Choose whether the channel should be public or private</li>
      <li>Click <strong>Create Channel</strong></li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Joining a Channel</h3>
    <p>To join a public channel:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Click <strong>Browse all channels</strong> in the sidebar</li>
      <li>Find the channel you want to join</li>
      <li>Click <strong>Join</strong></li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Leaving a Channel</h3>
    <p>To leave a channel, open the channel and click the <strong>Leave</strong> option from the channel menu.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- User Guide: Direct Messages -->
  <section id="direct-messages">
    <h2>Direct Messages</h2>
    <p>Direct messages (DMs) allow you to have private one-on-one conversations with other users.</p>

    <h3>Starting a DM</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Click the <strong>+</strong> button next to "Direct Messages" in the sidebar</li>
      <li>Search for and select the user you want to message</li>
      <li>Start typing your message</li>
    </ol>
    <p>You can also start a DM by clicking on a user's name anywhere in the app.</p>

    <h3>DM Privacy</h3>
    <p>Direct messages are private conversations between you and one other user. They are not visible to anyone else, including administrators.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- User Guide: Messages -->
  <section id="messages">
    <h2>Messages</h2>
    <p>Learn about the various features available when sending and managing messages.</p>

    <h3>Sending Messages</h3>
    <p>Type your message in the text field at the bottom of the channel or DM, then press <kbd>Enter</kbd> or click the send button.</p>

    <h3>Mentions</h3>
    <p>To get someone's attention, mention them by typing <code>@</code> followed by their username. They'll receive a notification about your message.</p>

    <h3>File Attachments</h3>
    <p>You can attach files to your messages:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Click the attachment icon in the message input</li>
      <li>Select the file(s) you want to upload</li>
      <li>Add an optional message</li>
      <li>Send your message</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Supported file types include images (JPEG, PNG, GIF, WebP), documents, and other common formats.</p>

    <h3>Search</h3>
    <p>Use the search feature to find messages across all your channels and DMs. Press <kbd>Cmd</kbd>+<kbd>K</kbd> (Mac) or <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>K</kbd> (Windows/Linux) to open the command palette and search.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- User Guide: Profile Settings -->
  <section id="profile-settings">
    <h2>Profile & Settings</h2>
    <p>Customize your profile and manage your account settings.</p>

    <h3>Updating Your Profile</h3>
    <p>To update your profile:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Click on your avatar in the bottom-left corner</li>
      <li>Select <strong>Settings</strong></li>
      <li>Update your username, avatar, timezone, or status</li>
      <li>Click <strong>Save Changes</strong></li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Changing Your Avatar</h3>
    <p>You can upload a custom avatar image:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Supported formats: JPEG, PNG, GIF, WebP</li>
      <li>Maximum file size: 5MB</li>
      <li>Recommended size: 200x200 pixels or larger</li>
    </ul>
    <p>If you don't upload an avatar, your initials will be displayed with a colored background.</p>

    <h3>Changing Your Password</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Go to Settings</li>
      <li>Navigate to the Password section</li>
      <li>Enter your current password</li>
      <li>Enter and confirm your new password</li>
      <li>Click <strong>Update Password</strong></li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <!-- User Guide: Two-Factor Auth -->
  <section id="two-factor-auth">
    <h2>Two-Factor Authentication</h2>
    <p>Two-factor authentication (2FA) adds an extra layer of security to your account.</p>

    <h3>Setting Up 2FA</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Go to Settings > Security</li>
      <li>Click <strong>Enable Two-Factor Authentication</strong></li>
      <li>Scan the QR code with your authenticator app (Google Authenticator, Authy, etc.)</li>
      <li>Enter the 6-digit code from your app to verify</li>
      <li>Save your backup codes in a secure location</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Backup Codes</h3>
    <p>When you enable 2FA, you'll receive 10 backup codes. Each code can only be used once. Store these codes securely - you'll need them if you lose access to your authenticator app.</p>
    <p>You can regenerate backup codes at any time from the Security settings, which will invalidate all previous codes.</p>

    <h3>Disabling 2FA</h3>
    <p>To disable two-factor authentication:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Go to Settings > Security</li>
      <li>Click <strong>Disable Two-Factor Authentication</strong></li>
      <li>Confirm by entering a verification code or backup code</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <!-- User Guide: Mobile Setup -->
  <section id="mobile-setup">
    <h2>Mobile App Setup</h2>
    <p>Connect your mobile device to your HomeChat server using the iOS or Android app.</p>

    <h3>Connecting Your Device</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Install the HomeChat app on your mobile device</li>
      <li>On the web interface, go to Settings > Mobile Setup</li>
      <li>A QR code will be displayed</li>
      <li>Open the mobile app and scan the QR code</li>
      <li>Your device will automatically connect to the server</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>PWA Installation</h3>
    <p>HomeChat can also be installed as a Progressive Web App (PWA) directly from your browser:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>iOS Safari</strong>: Tap the Share button, then "Add to Home Screen"</li>
      <li><strong>Android Chrome</strong>: Tap the menu, then "Install app" or "Add to Home Screen"</li>
      <li><strong>Desktop Chrome/Edge</strong>: Click the install icon in the address bar</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- Admin Guide: Server Settings -->
  <section id="server-settings">
    <h2>Server Settings</h2>
    <p>Configure your HomeChat server. These settings are only accessible to administrators.</p>

    <h3>General Settings</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Setting</th><th>Description</th><th>Default</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Site Name</strong></td><td>Displayed in the header and browser title</td><td>HomeChat</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Allow Signups</strong></td><td>Enable/disable new user registration</td><td>Enabled</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Require Approval</strong></td><td>New users must be approved by an admin</td><td>Disabled</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>API Settings</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Setting</th><th>Description</th><th>Default</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Enable REST API</strong></td><td>Allow external integrations via the API</td><td>Enabled</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Enable Home Assistant</strong></td><td>Enable Home Assistant integration features</td><td>Enabled</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Webhook Base URL</strong></td><td>Base URL for webhook callbacks (auto-detected if blank)</td><td>Auto</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <!-- Admin Guide: User Management -->
  <section id="user-management">
    <h2>User Management</h2>
    <p>Manage users, approve registrations, and assign roles.</p>

    <h3>Approving Users</h3>
    <p>If <strong>Require Approval</strong> is enabled, new registrations will appear in the Admin > Users section with a "Pending" status.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Go to Admin > Users</li>
      <li>Find users with "Pending approval" status</li>
      <li>Click <strong>Approve</strong> to grant access or <strong>Reject</strong> to deny</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>User Roles</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>User</strong> - Standard access to channels, DMs, and personal settings</li>
      <li><strong>Admin</strong> - Full access including server settings, user management, and bot configuration</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Account Lockout</h3>
    <p>For security, accounts are automatically locked after 5 failed login attempts. The lockout lasts 15 minutes. Administrators can manually unlock accounts from the Users section.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- Admin Guide: PWA Configuration -->
  <section id="pwa-configuration">
    <h2>PWA Configuration</h2>
    <p>Customize the Progressive Web App settings for your HomeChat installation.</p>

    <h3>PWA Settings</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Setting</th><th>Description</th><th>Default</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Enable PWA</strong></td><td>Allow users to install HomeChat as an app</td><td>Enabled</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Short Name</strong></td><td>Name shown on home screens and launchers</td><td>HomeChat</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Theme Color</strong></td><td>Browser toolbar and title bar color</td><td>#2563eb</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Background Color</strong></td><td>Splash screen background color</td><td>#ffffff</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Display Mode</strong></td><td>How the app appears when launched</td><td>Standalone</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>Custom Icons</h3>
    <p>To customize the app icon, replace the files at:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><code>/icon.png</code> - PNG icon (recommended: 512x512)</li>
      <li><code>/icon.svg</code> - SVG icon for high-resolution displays</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- Admin Guide: Push Notifications -->
  <section id="push-notifications">
    <h2>Push Notifications</h2>
    <p>Configure Firebase Cloud Messaging (FCM) to enable push notifications on mobile devices.</p>

    <h3>Setting Up FCM</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Create a project in the <a href="https://console.firebase.google.com/" target="_blank">Firebase Console</a></li>
      <li>Go to Project Settings > Service Accounts</li>
      <li>Click "Generate new private key" to download your credentials</li>
      <li>In HomeChat Admin > Settings > Notifications, enter:
        <ul>
          <li>Project ID</li>
          <li>Client Email</li>
          <li>Private Key</li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li>Enable push notifications</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Testing Notifications</h3>
    <p>Once configured, notifications will be sent automatically when:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>A user receives a direct message</li>
      <li>A user is mentioned in a channel</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- Admin Guide: AI Configuration -->
  <section id="ai-configuration">
    <h2>AI Configuration</h2>
    <p>Configure LiteLLM to enable AI-powered bots in your HomeChat server.</p>

    <h3>LiteLLM Setup</h3>
    <p>HomeChat uses <a href="https://litellm.ai/" target="_blank">LiteLLM</a> as a proxy to connect to various AI model providers.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Set up a LiteLLM server (self-hosted or cloud)</li>
      <li>In Admin > Settings > AI, configure:
        <ul>
          <li><strong>LiteLLM Host</strong> - URL of your LiteLLM instance (e.g., <code>http://localhost:4000</code>)</li>
          <li><strong>API Key</strong> - Your LiteLLM API key</li>
          <li><strong>Default Model</strong> - Model to use when not specified per-bot</li>
        </ul>
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Available Models</h3>
    <p>Once connected, HomeChat will fetch available models from your LiteLLM instance. You can select a default model and override it per-bot when creating AI bots.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- Automations: Bots Overview -->
  <section id="bots-overview">
    <h2>Bots Overview</h2>
    <p>HomeChat supports different types of bots for automation and integration.</p>

    <h3>Bot Types</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Type</th><th>Description</th><th>Use Case</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Webhook</strong></td><td>Receives HTTP webhooks from external services</td><td>Notifications from CI/CD, monitoring systems</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>API</strong></td><td>Integrates via REST API with token auth</td><td>Custom integrations, scripts</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>AI</strong></td><td>Autonomous chat bot using LLM</td><td>Assistant, Q&A, conversation</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>Managing Bots</h3>
    <p>All bot management is done through Admin > Automations:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>View all configured bots</li>
      <li>Create new bots</li>
      <li>Activate or deactivate bots</li>
      <li>Delete bots</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- Automations: Webhook Bots -->
  <section id="webhook-bots">
    <h2>Webhook Bots</h2>
    <p>Webhook bots receive HTTP POST requests from external services and post messages to HomeChat.</p>

    <h3>Creating a Webhook Bot</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Go to Admin > Automations</li>
      <li>Click <strong>New Bot</strong></li>
      <li>Select "Webhook" as the bot type</li>
      <li>Enter a name and optional description</li>
      <li>Save the bot</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Webhook URL</h3>
    <p>Each webhook bot has a unique URL:</p>
    <pre class="docs-code">POST /api/v1/webhooks/{webhook_id}</pre>

    <h3>Authentication</h3>
    <p>Webhook requests are authenticated using HMAC-SHA256 signatures. Include the signature in the <code>X-Hub-Signature-256</code> header:</p>
    <pre class="docs-code">X-Hub-Signature-256: sha256={signature}</pre>
    <p>Calculate the signature by computing HMAC-SHA256 of the request body using the webhook secret.</p>

    <h3>Payload Format</h3>
    <pre class="docs-code">{
  "action": "send_message",
  "channel_id": 1,
  "content": "Hello from webhook!"
}</pre>
  </section>

  <!-- Automations: AI Bots -->
  <section id="ai-bots">
    <h2>AI Bots</h2>
    <p>AI bots use large language models to participate in conversations autonomously.</p>

    <h3>Creating an AI Bot</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Ensure LiteLLM is configured in Settings > AI</li>
      <li>Go to Admin > Automations</li>
      <li>Click <strong>New AI Bot</strong></li>
      <li>Configure the bot:
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Name</strong> - Bot's display name (3-50 characters)</li>
          <li><strong>Description</strong> - Optional description</li>
          <li><strong>Instructions</strong> - System prompt that defines the bot's personality and behavior</li>
          <li><strong>Model</strong> - LLM model to use (or leave blank for default)</li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li>Save and activate the bot</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Bot Behavior</h3>
    <p>AI bots automatically respond when:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>They are mentioned with <code>@botname</code></li>
      <li>They receive a direct message</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Writing Good Instructions</h3>
    <p>The instructions field is the system prompt for the LLM. Good instructions should:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Define the bot's role and personality</li>
      <li>Specify any constraints or guidelines</li>
      <li>Include relevant context about what the bot should know</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- Automations: API Tokens -->
  <section id="api-tokens">
    <h2>API Tokens</h2>
    <p>API tokens authenticate external applications and integrations with your HomeChat server.</p>

    <h3>Creating a Token</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Go to Admin > Automations > API Tokens tab</li>
      <li>Enter a descriptive name for the token</li>
      <li>Click <strong>Create Token</strong></li>
      <li>Copy the token immediately - it won't be shown again</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Using Tokens</h3>
    <p>Include the token in API requests using the Authorization header:</p>
    <pre class="docs-code">Authorization: Bearer {your_token}</pre>

    <h3>Token Security</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Tokens are hashed and cannot be retrieved after creation</li>
      <li>Create separate tokens for each integration</li>
      <li>Deactivate or delete tokens that are no longer needed</li>
      <li>Regenerate tokens if you suspect they've been compromised</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- API Reference: Authentication -->
  <section id="api-authentication">
    <h2>API Authentication</h2>
    <p>HomeChat API supports multiple authentication methods.</p>

    <h3>Token Authentication</h3>
    <p>For external integrations, use API tokens:</p>
    <pre class="docs-code">curl -X GET "https://your-server/api/v1/channels" \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer {your_token}"</pre>

    <h3>Session Authentication</h3>
    <p>For user-facing applications, use session-based authentication:</p>
    <pre class="docs-code"># Sign in
POST /api/v1/signin
{
  "username": "your_username",
  "password": "your_password"
}

# Response
{
  "user": { ... },
  "token": "session_token"
}</pre>

    <h3>Two-Factor Authentication Flow</h3>
    <p>If 2FA is enabled, the sign-in response will indicate verification is required:</p>
    <pre class="docs-code"># Sign in response with 2FA
{
  "requires_2fa": true,
  "temp_token": "temporary_token"
}

# Verify 2FA
POST /api/v1/signin/verify_2fa
{
  "temp_token": "temporary_token",
  "code": "123456"
}</pre>
  </section>

